Chapter: GopDraw
  cites Kernel chapter BitmapFont

 Drawing primitives for the GOP framebuffer on the Option A boot path.
 The framebuffer is linear 32-bit XRGB; base and stride (pixels per
 scan line) come from the boot stub's handoff cells. Text renders from
 the CBF bitmap font: each glyph is 8x16, and every on-pixel is drawn
 as a scale-by-scale square, so the same font renders 8x16 at scale 1
 and 16x32 at scale 2. Panels 1024 pixels wide or wider use scale 2.

 We say:

Section: Cursor

  cursor-w : Integer = 16
  cursor-h : Integer = 24

  cursor-col-edge : Integer = 1052688
  cursor-col-fill : Integer = 16777215

 The arrow, one 16-bit mask per row, bit 15 leftmost. Two masks rather than
 one because a pointer has to stay visible over both a dark wall and a lit
 panel: the edge is drawn dark, the fill white, and a pixel in neither is left
 alone so the background shows through beside the tail.

 IT IS 16 BY 24 AND IS DRAWN AT ONE DEVICE PIXEL PER MASK BIT. It was 8 by 12
 block-scaled by `ui-scale`, so at 1600 and above every mask bit became a 2 by
 2 square and the diagonal came out as a staircase of blocks. Damian,
 2026-08-26: crisp up that pointer. The device footprint is UNCHANGED at those
 sizes -- 8 by 12 doubled is the same 16 by 24 -- so the pointer is the same
 size on the glass with four times the detail, and the save-under buffer,
 whose row stride is already 16, needs no more room than it always did.

   r0   E...............   r12  EFFFFFFFFFFFE...
   r1   EE..............   r13  EFFFFFFFFFFFFE..
   r2   EFE.............   r14  EFFFFFFFFFFFFFE.
   r3   EFFE............   r15  EFFFFFFFFEEEEEEE
   r4   EFFFE...........   r16  EFFFFFFFFE......
   r5   EFFFFE..........   r17  EFFFFEFFFE......
   r6   EFFFFFE.........   r18  EFFFE.EFFFE.....
   r7   EFFFFFFE........   r19  EFFE...EFFFE....
   r8   EFFFFFFFE.......   r20  EFE.....EFFFE...
   r9   EFFFFFFFFE......   r21  EE.......EFFFE..
   r10  EFFFFFFFFFE.....   r22  E.........EFFE..
   r11  EFFFFFFFFFFE....   r23  ...........EEE..

 These are functions rather than a `List Integer` constant, and that is not
 style. A top-level list constant is rebuilt at every mention
 (DevelopersGuide, `sha256-h0`), so `list-at cursor-mask r` inside the pixel
 loop would allocate a fresh list per pixel, on a routine that runs on every
 mouse move with no GC underneath it. The row masks are also hoisted out of
 the column loop, so the comparison chain runs once per row rather than once
 per pixel.

